    "datasource": "Kodak Professional Portra 160NC, 160VC, 400NC, 400VC and 800 Films, publication E-190 (page 11); SHA256 be962705c3beaf795d82e1a7dd9d3304edb3eed7a42e9aed7a999ef7190be876. Reproduce with scripts/bw_expansion/prepare_colour_film.py; axis calibration derived from the page by scripts/bw_expansion/extract_graphs.py; source points, assumptions and conditions: data/profile-sources/kodak-portra-400vc/recipe.json. Dye spectra: kodak_portra_400 shapes, scaled so their mixture reproduces this sheet's own midscale-neutral-minus-D-min. PCHIP of the three plotted log-sensitivity traces; engine covers 380-780 nm, each trace is used only over its own plotted span and is unmeasured elsewhere. Relative shape only; the panel's E.N.D. density criterion is not an absolute speed. Measured, not assumed: the sheet's D-min trace is the spectral base density, and what the Status M densitometer reads through it is subtracted from the characteristic curves. The first stock here whose base is not a flat guess. Spectral adaptation parameters are not this stock's own: they are borrowed from kodak_portra_400. Same family, same process. Density curves balanced against a rendered 5-stop grey ramp by scripts/bw_expansion/balance_against_ramp.py (upstream's correct_positive_curves_with_gray_ramp): density scale [0.9213, 0.9201, 0.9225], log-exposure shift [-0.0065, 0.0, 0.0], data_trustability 0.2. Worst channel deviation from neutral across the ramp 9.5% before, 9.1% after. This correction is fitted against the engine, not measured from the datasheet."
